    | xargs -0 sha256sum | awk '{print $1}' | LC_ALL=C sort | sha256sum

  repos/npm is an on-demand cache rather than a bulk index, so it grows when a
  run asks for a name it lacks; eval/npm/seed.sh deliberately fills a symlink
  farm under the run directory instead, leaving this tree untouched, and the
  measured sweeps then run against a frozen shim.  A changed hash here means
  something wrote into the checkout.

  What --fill fetches lands in that farm, not here, so it has to be copied
  back to be pinned.  3829 packuments were fetched 2026-09-10/11; the other
  163 are what seed.sh's --fill fetched on 2026-09-20 for names npm asked for
  and the snapshot lacked, copied here from the farm unchanged -- pac reads
  them as well, through --cache, so without them the sweep reproduces only
  from one machine's /tmp.  A farm built from this tree by setup.sh alone
  reproduces every recorded npm score.  Tarballs, should a goal ever need
  one, land in tarballs/ beside the farm and must be pinned the same way; no
  goal fetches one today, since npm asks for a tarball only for a version
  that bundles or carries a shrinkwrap, and eval/npm/check-misses.sh fails a
  frozen run that asks for anything the farm lacks.

The cargo sweeps need nothing pinned beyond repos/crates.io-index above: both
of them ask cargo only for a lockfile, which it resolves from index rows alone
and writes without downloading a single crate body.  There is no CARGO_HOME
cache in the universe they answer about, and so none to record here.
